ia-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">N</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"nDXI5E7AGm"},{"type":"text","value":" due to spin-canted antiferromagnetism (Frederichs et al., 2003). If a sample is cooled in zero field to low temperature, given a saturation isothermal remanent magnetization (SIRM) at ~5–10 K, and then warmed (a zero-field-cooled or ZFC remanence experiment), the ~37 K transition causes a loss of magnetization (","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"YvJ6hVylmp"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"siderite_fc_zfc_rtsirm","label":"siderite_FC_ZFC_RTSIRM","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"erOZ5TXuWW"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"XF47RH2lSg"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"siderite-fc-zfc-rtsirm","key":"p9pMIUndnI"},{"type":"text","value":"). The similarity between ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"MqsCXr9ga2"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"T_\\mathrm{N}","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">N</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"RBrctYFRhH"},{"type":"text","value":" of siderite and rhodochrosite can make it difficult to distinguish the two based on the transition temperature alone (Dekkers et al., 2023). If the sample is cooled from room-temperatures in the presence of a strong field (field-cooled, FC) before the remanence is measured the magnetization acquired (and then subsequently lost upon warming) is much larger than in a ZFC experiment (","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"sKpxU4Nj8a"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"siderite_fc_zfc_rtsirm","label":"siderite_FC_ZFC_RTSIRM","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"SfkAwfrHKn"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"UZie7tbJvc"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"siderite-fc-zfc-rtsirm","key":"OutmxVcf9A"},{"type":"text","value":"). This pronounced divergence between the FC and the ZFC curves (with FC>ZFC) is a hallmark of siderite (","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"MAhoSQeWKg"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"siderite_fc_zfc_rtsirm","label":"siderite_FC_ZFC_RTSIRM","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"CYyGaM124d"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"E1l8Qawuk7"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"siderite-fc-zfc-rtsirm","key":"kxAOex00lL"},{"type":"text","value":"). This large difference is diagnostic for siderite relative to rhodochrosite and vivianite with a FC/ZFC ratio of 3 or greater proposed to identify siderite (Dekkers et al., 2023).","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"UaIMzBUBWS"}],"key":"BTolaS4fS1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This FC to ZFC difference also plays an important role in distinguishing siderite’s transition from the Besnus transition of pyrrhotite that occurs at a similar temperature Housen et al., 1996. Monoclinic pyrrhotite does not show a remanence divergence across in FC/ZFC data at its 32–34 K transition. Pyrrhotite’s low-temperature transition is a crystallographic/anisotropy change, but it remains a ferromagnetic (ferrimagnetic) mineral throughout, and experiments have found that ZFC vs. FC warming curves for pyrrhotite are nearly superposed in magnitude (Horng and Roberts, 2018).","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"nEYjpsI09I"}],"key":"FNKWz9MQXZ"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"References","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"RpBzcgIKww"}],"identifier":"references","label":"References","html_id":"references","implicit":true,"key":"UTUIpmUROH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark J. Dekkers, Wout Hanckmann, Simo Spassov, and Thilo Behre